1
00:00:03,044 --> 00:00:23,071
[SPEAKER_00]: So, at one time, the Buddha was staying in the land of Malas, near the Malian town named Uruvelakapa.

2
00:00:24,565 --> 00:00:29,106
[SPEAKER_00]: Once the Buddha woke up in the morning, taking his bowl and robe, he entered the town for alms.

3
00:00:29,306 --> 00:00:32,827
[SPEAKER_00]: After the meal, on his return, he addressed Venerable Ananda.

4
00:00:34,748 --> 00:00:39,069
[SPEAKER_00]: Ananda, you stay right here, while I plunge deep into the great wood for the days abiding.

5
00:00:40,550 --> 00:00:52,533
[SPEAKER_00]: So, while Ananda was alone, the householder Tapusa approached him, sat down to one side and said, Bhante Ananda, we lay people

6
00:00:55,269 --> 00:00:57,291
[SPEAKER_00]: We are lay people who partake in sensuality.

7
00:00:58,231 --> 00:01:02,375
[SPEAKER_00]: We enjoy sensuality, we are fond of it and are pleased by it.

8
00:01:04,096 --> 00:01:11,442
[SPEAKER_00]: To us lay people who partake in sensuality, who enjoy it, are fond of it and are pleased by it, renunciation seems like an abyss.

9
00:01:13,584 --> 00:01:19,228
[SPEAKER_00]: I have heard that in this teaching and discipline, there are very young bhikkhus whose minds leap at renunciation.

10
00:01:19,448 --> 00:01:23,552
[SPEAKER_00]: Their minds, seeing it as peaceful, are confident, settled and liberated in it.

11
00:01:24,958 --> 00:01:32,023
[SPEAKER_00]: It is precisely the renunciation that is dividing line between the many and the because in this teaching and discipline.

12
00:01:33,324 --> 00:01:46,814
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like... you can notice here that not just the difference in partaking in sensuality and mind leaping towards renunciation has been highlighted here.

13
00:01:46,914 --> 00:01:48,655
[SPEAKER_00]: It was also highlighted by a householder.

14
00:01:48,736 --> 00:01:51,518
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he fully understood

15
00:01:52,653 --> 00:01:57,875
[SPEAKER_00]: that the main difference, as he said, the dividing line between the many, i.e.

16
00:01:58,055 --> 00:02:12,441
[SPEAKER_00]: the outsiders, the lay people, the other practices, the other teachings, so the main differences between the many, the manifold, and these, because in this teaching and discipline is this precisely this renunciation.

17
00:02:13,657 --> 00:02:18,681
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not like that there were no other samanas and brāhmaṇas.

18
00:02:18,821 --> 00:02:22,965
[SPEAKER_00]: They were practicing ascetic practices and renunciation to some extent.

19
00:02:23,045 --> 00:02:33,714
[SPEAKER_00]: But, in this particular way, in this teaching and discipline, renunciation does require knowing where to apply it.

20
00:02:33,934 --> 00:02:39,478
[SPEAKER_00]: So, not to just turn into blind asceticism or the external form worship or similar.

21
00:02:40,699 --> 00:02:41,220
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eh...

22
00:02:42,864 --> 00:02:52,749
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, that would then bring understanding, as we're going to see further in the sutta, which is like why then the mind gets to see it as peaceful and it's confident and settled and liberated in it.

23
00:02:53,609 --> 00:02:57,411
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the layman understood that the very sensuality...

24
00:02:58,291 --> 00:03:04,034
[SPEAKER_00]: So, abandoning the sensuality on the right level is the difference between everybody else and the Buddha's teaching.

25
00:03:06,047 --> 00:03:15,354
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, eh... the dividing line between many and the big is precisely this renunciation in regard to sensuality and sensual desires.

26
00:03:16,675 --> 00:03:25,562
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he could have added here, oh yes, and Jains as well, they have all sorts of austere practices, but they still don't abandon sensuality, and that was also the Buddha's point.

27
00:03:27,063 --> 00:03:32,788
[SPEAKER_00]: Saying no to things blindly, not recognizing that the root or the gateway

28
00:03:33,708 --> 00:03:45,097
[SPEAKER_00]: for sensuality and your desires, it's on the level of your intentions, and then, not cultivating it accordingly, means even if you renounce everything to the extreme, you're still within the central domain.

29
00:03:48,580 --> 00:03:49,420
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, it goes further.

30
00:03:49,921 --> 00:03:53,203
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the Ananda says, okay, well, let's see the Buddha about this matter.

31
00:03:54,464 --> 00:03:57,347
[SPEAKER_00]: So, as he answers, so we shall remember it.

32
00:03:59,588 --> 00:04:08,853
[SPEAKER_00]: So, together they went to the Buddha, bowed and sat to one side, and then the Venerable Ananda reported this conversation, and the Buddha said, that's exactly how it is.

33
00:04:09,374 --> 00:04:26,643
[SPEAKER_00]: Before my awakening, when I was still an unawakened, but intent on awakening, I too thought renunciation is good, seclusion is good, but the mind, citta, didn't see renunciation as peaceful.

34
00:04:28,826 --> 00:04:34,889
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the Buddha realized, okay, so why is this the case?

35
00:04:35,069 --> 00:04:45,094
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do I see, on one level, that renunciation is beneficial, seclusion is beneficial, but the mind is not leaping towards it?

36
00:04:45,114 --> 00:04:46,795
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he said, I thought, what is the cause?

37
00:04:47,455 --> 00:04:51,497
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the reason why my mind doesn't see renunciation as peaceful and it doesn't leap at it?

38
00:04:51,637 --> 00:04:53,738
[SPEAKER_00]: It isn't confident, settled and liberated in it.

39
00:04:55,516 --> 00:05:04,010
[SPEAKER_00]: And he realized that he hasn't seen the peril of sensuality and hasn't cultivated that.

40
00:05:04,150 --> 00:05:08,597
[SPEAKER_00]: He hasn't realized the value of renunciation and didn't foster it.

41
00:05:09,626 --> 00:05:16,648
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why the mind doesn't see renunciation as peaceful and doesn't leap at it and it isn't confident and settled and liberated in it.

42
00:05:17,449 --> 00:05:33,514
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this is obviously the kind of the crucial point because, you know, he could have said million of different practices that could lead you to see peril in sensuality because of which your mind is not leaping at it, but he didn't.

43
00:05:35,006 --> 00:05:37,687
[SPEAKER_00]: He always highlighted the same thing.

44
00:05:38,207 --> 00:05:40,768
[SPEAKER_00]: Not having seen the danger sufficiently enough.

45
00:05:41,689 --> 00:05:54,014
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, there are many suttas in Anguttara as well, that keep repeating that if the one hasn't seen the danger, in it, the mind is still within the domain, not free from the hindrances, thus devoid of jhāna and everything else.

46
00:05:54,674 --> 00:06:03,558
[SPEAKER_00]: So, as we're gonna see later on, when we address the more specific questions, the point is, there is no other way of going about it.

47
00:06:04,844 --> 00:06:17,183
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, nothing can magically reveal you the peril of sensuality, except pondering, thinking and trying to understand the extent of the peril of the sensuality.

48
00:06:18,982 --> 00:06:20,143
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what he said.

49
00:06:20,203 --> 00:06:20,864
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the cause?

50
00:06:20,904 --> 00:06:26,229
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the cause is that I haven't seen the peril of sensuality, haven't cultivated it, hasn't realized the value.

51
00:06:26,970 --> 00:06:30,694
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why the mind is not... doesn't see renunciation as peaceful.

52
00:06:31,434 --> 00:06:39,923
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, I thought, suppose that, having seen the peril of sensuality, I were to cultivate that, to not lose the context of that, to not distract myself from that, to not forget it.

53
00:06:41,395 --> 00:06:44,437
[SPEAKER_00]: I suppose that, having realized the value of renunciation, I were to foster it.

54
00:06:45,078 --> 00:06:52,684
[SPEAKER_00]: It is possible that, seeing it as peaceful, my mind would then leap at renunciation and be confident, settled and liberated.

55
00:06:53,525 --> 00:07:00,510
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, after some time, having seen the peril of sensuality, I cultivated that, having realized the value of renunciation, I fostered it.

56
00:07:02,351 --> 00:07:06,715
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, seeing it as peaceful, my mind leapt at renunciation and was confident, settled and liberated in it.

57
00:07:07,703 --> 00:07:20,366
[SPEAKER_00]: Having... and so, having thoroughly withdrawn from sensuality, having withdrawn from unbeneficial phenomena, with thinking and pondering, with joy and ease, bored of that withdrawal, I abided having entered upon the first jhāna.

58
00:07:21,446 --> 00:07:23,307
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there was no middleman there.

59
00:07:23,327 --> 00:07:24,627
[SPEAKER_00]: There was no...

60
00:07:24,647 --> 00:07:32,089
[SPEAKER_00]: I was massaging my breath until the pleasure overwhelmed me and that brought me to the jhāna and wanted... and took my mind away from sensuality.

61
00:07:33,022 --> 00:07:38,964
[SPEAKER_00]: And, by the way, what this paragraph, this passage describes, it's just found in all the other suttas.

62
00:07:39,404 --> 00:07:46,607
[SPEAKER_00]: Whenever jhānas are described, they're described as arising on account of the condition of being thoroughly withdrawn from the five hindrances.

63
00:07:47,227 --> 00:07:50,048
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, sensuality being one of the chief of them.

64
00:07:50,863 --> 00:08:06,533
[SPEAKER_00]: The point being, the Buddha didn't say, oh, through practicing such concentration or such shortcut or such management technique, I took myself away from the sensuality that I knew it was bad, but my mind wasn't listening to me.

65
00:08:06,553 --> 00:08:06,693
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

66
00:08:07,273 --> 00:08:15,158
[SPEAKER_00]: First, I basically taught my mind the peril of it.

67
00:08:16,648 --> 00:08:18,530
[SPEAKER_00]: I've seen it for myself, first and foremost.

68
00:08:18,970 --> 00:08:20,031
[SPEAKER_00]: I've seen the extent of it.

69
00:08:20,311 --> 00:08:24,215
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's when he says that he cultivated it.

70
00:08:24,455 --> 00:08:28,920
[SPEAKER_00]: So, one thing is to understand the peril on the level of your mano and intellect.

71
00:08:29,727 --> 00:08:31,928
[SPEAKER_00]: Through pondering, through abandoning of the wrong views.

72
00:08:32,568 --> 00:08:39,710
[SPEAKER_00]: But another thing is for that value of that renunciation of sensuality, through seeing the peril, to sink in.

73
00:08:40,010 --> 00:08:47,873
[SPEAKER_00]: To sink in down to that wild animal, so that, as I said before, the wild animal will then also prefer renunciation, not sensuality.

74
00:08:48,313 --> 00:08:55,175
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not enough to just counter your moods by a subha or to tell yourself this is not worthy when you feel attracted.

75
00:08:55,515 --> 00:08:57,256
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all on the level of your intellect.

76
00:08:57,928 --> 00:09:03,431
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, you should be doing it, but don't confuse that for being the practice of training the citta.

77
00:09:04,452 --> 00:09:06,973
[SPEAKER_00]: In order to train the citta, the thing needs to sink in.

78
00:09:07,514 --> 00:09:08,134
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what it means.

79
00:09:08,394 --> 00:09:11,756
[SPEAKER_00]: Having realized the value of renunciation, he fostered it.

80
00:09:13,077 --> 00:09:22,903
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, he maintained it, he protected it, he clarified it further, he sustained it, until he saw that his own mind now is leaping towards it, actually wants it.

81
00:09:23,938 --> 00:09:30,504
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, being... that basically constitutes the withdrawal from the sensuality.

82
00:09:30,564 --> 00:09:35,268
[SPEAKER_00]: Not your reasoning and your intellect simply knowing, oh, it's bad, thus I'm withdrawn.

83
00:09:36,029 --> 00:09:39,572
[SPEAKER_00]: When the mind doesn't want to do it, you feel relieved now.

84
00:09:40,333 --> 00:09:45,157
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that implicit, as others sort of say, that intimate friend that always kind of

85
00:09:46,852 --> 00:09:50,775
[SPEAKER_00]: It has potential to send you or cause you the danger to your life.

86
00:09:50,855 --> 00:09:51,535
[SPEAKER_00]: That's gone now.

87
00:09:51,635 --> 00:09:55,458
[SPEAKER_00]: Because he wants the same thing as you yourself have understood is beneficial.

88
00:09:56,598 --> 00:09:58,039
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, it's the first jhāna.

89
00:09:59,600 --> 00:10:00,361
[SPEAKER_00]: One and only way.

90
00:10:00,421 --> 00:10:02,062
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no other way to jhāna.

91
00:10:03,683 --> 00:10:04,403
[SPEAKER_00]: Through withdrawal.

92
00:10:04,864 --> 00:10:07,725
[SPEAKER_00]: And the withdrawal is only possible on account of seeing the peril.

93
00:10:09,026 --> 00:10:11,368
[SPEAKER_00]: Otherwise, the Buddha wouldn't have said that very thing.

94
00:10:13,045 --> 00:10:15,967
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he abided in the first jhāna, says, So, that was in the first jhāna.

95
00:10:31,918 --> 00:10:46,182
[SPEAKER_00]: So, withdrawn from the first jhāna, surmounting the domain, the entire domain of sensuality, what you're going to have in front, so to speak, will be attentions and perceptions that come from that central domain.

96
00:10:47,923 --> 00:10:58,346
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're withdrawn, there is the ease of safety, the pleasure, the joy on account of safety, but the fact is those things are there, still, in front.

97
00:10:59,400 --> 00:11:03,343
[SPEAKER_00]: And then he would realize, oh, this is not, well, not as peaceful as it could be.

98
00:11:04,904 --> 00:11:07,125
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly why he then went to a second jhāna.

99
00:11:07,585 --> 00:11:20,633
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in other words, the joy of the first jhāna, the withdrawal, the absolute wholesomeness, as the other suttas say, there is nothing unwholesome that, eh... that's sort of, eh... implicit in the first jhāna.

100
00:11:20,673 --> 00:11:22,815
[SPEAKER_00]: It's fully divorced from everything unwholesome.

101
00:11:23,660 --> 00:11:30,183
[SPEAKER_00]: But, at the arm's length, there is still that annoying, afflicting, sensual domain.

102
00:11:31,424 --> 00:11:34,545
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're not directly afflicted by it, but it's there in front of you.

103
00:11:34,645 --> 00:11:39,147
[SPEAKER_00]: And it says, those perceptions beset the mind through thinking and pondering, pretty much.

104
00:11:40,208 --> 00:11:45,790
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why he thought, how about I appease or I surmount the thinking and pondering?

105
00:11:46,031 --> 00:11:47,731
[SPEAKER_00]: How about I withdraw even further?

106
00:11:47,971 --> 00:11:48,832
[SPEAKER_00]: In the same sense,

107
00:11:51,024 --> 00:12:13,073
[SPEAKER_00]: He realized the peril, the danger, the dissatisfaction, on that level of his mano, on that mental level, on the level of understanding and the view, and then cultivated it, fostered it, thought about it, contemplated it, meditated it, until it sank down, sank in, into his citta.

108
00:12:13,633 --> 00:12:15,034
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the citta turned away from it.

109
00:12:15,494 --> 00:12:17,135
[SPEAKER_00]: How about he repeats the same?

110
00:12:18,004 --> 00:12:27,348
[SPEAKER_00]: regards thinking and pondering as unbeneficial, as dangerous, as perilous, as not peaceful enough, until the citta turns away from that.

111
00:12:28,369 --> 00:12:30,510
[SPEAKER_00]: And it repeats the same the same formula.

112
00:12:31,350 --> 00:12:38,013
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, why don't I appease the thinking and abide in the higher, basically, surmounting, higher comprehension.

113
00:12:38,734 --> 00:12:41,355
[SPEAKER_00]: He says, but my mind did not see the absence of thinking as peaceful.

114
00:12:42,878 --> 00:12:46,259
[SPEAKER_00]: In the same sense, he didn't see before renunciation, i.e.

115
00:12:46,299 --> 00:12:48,579
[SPEAKER_00]: the absence of sensuality, didn't see it as peaceful.

116
00:12:49,080 --> 00:12:50,100
[SPEAKER_00]: He was actually afraid of it.

117
00:12:50,140 --> 00:12:53,241
[SPEAKER_00]: He was actually apprehensive about it, doubtful.

118
00:12:53,841 --> 00:12:56,361
[SPEAKER_00]: All sorts of ways, but he wasn't seeing it as peaceful.

119
00:12:57,382 --> 00:12:58,382
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can't just tell it.

120
00:12:58,782 --> 00:12:59,602
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to teach it.

121
00:12:59,622 --> 00:13:01,903
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to keep showing it through your own example.

122
00:13:02,063 --> 00:13:06,384
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, again, the virtue, sensory strength is already the example you're setting for your citta.

123
00:13:07,874 --> 00:13:13,919
[SPEAKER_00]: And this is, by the way, the most accurate, the most direct practice to the development of jhāna.

124
00:13:13,999 --> 00:13:16,522
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no clearer method than this.

125
00:13:18,123 --> 00:13:29,213
[SPEAKER_00]: Through clear pondering, thorough understanding of the danger, the peril of sensuality, the mind will leap towards renunciation.

126
00:13:29,273 --> 00:13:32,355
[SPEAKER_00]: It will lean away, it will turn away from sensual desires.

127
00:13:33,096 --> 00:13:35,338
[SPEAKER_00]: But not... who knows how long that will take.

128
00:13:37,088 --> 00:13:44,115
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, again, different people have carried, like, you know, different depths of their desires, to put it that way.

129
00:13:44,315 --> 00:13:46,737
[SPEAKER_00]: So, some people might... will need longer, some people not.

130
00:13:46,757 --> 00:13:56,827
[SPEAKER_00]: But, the point is, you won't be able to be conveying the message of the peril, the danger of sensuality, if you're still not withdrawn even through your physical behavior and verbal behavior and the coarse mental.

131
00:13:57,327 --> 00:13:58,748
[SPEAKER_00]: Thoughts and so on of indulgence.

132
00:13:59,349 --> 00:14:02,211
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that all... that's all practice of jhāna, basically.

133
00:14:02,291 --> 00:14:10,699
[SPEAKER_00]: From the day one to the actual jhāna, if you're doing it rightly, you are practicing the jhāna at any given moment of it.

134
00:14:10,759 --> 00:14:21,829
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then the Buddha realized that he again needs to repeat the same type of development of the context for his mind, so that the mind starts to see the absence of thinking as peaceful.

135
00:14:23,651 --> 00:14:28,936
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, he repeats the same and says on a later occasion, basically, he abided in this way.

136
00:14:28,977 --> 00:14:38,366
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, he recognized that perception and attention connected with thinking besetted him of that sensuality and there was an affliction.

137
00:14:38,526 --> 00:14:42,630
[SPEAKER_00]: So, suppose an unease would arise for someone who is at ease.

138
00:14:42,670 --> 00:14:47,135
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, he realized that, okay, so now that the absence of thinking

139
00:14:49,265 --> 00:14:56,211
[SPEAKER_00]: He developed, he wasn't bothered by the perceptions of sensuality like before, but now, in the second jhāna, he realized that becomes bothersome.

140
00:14:56,751 --> 00:15:03,376
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, it's not bothersome in a sense that, you know, in a sense that sensuality is perilous and bothersome.

141
00:15:03,416 --> 00:15:06,139
[SPEAKER_00]: Adi Sutta says that first jhāna is enough to become an arahant.

142
00:15:07,095 --> 00:15:15,083
[SPEAKER_00]: If you if you stay in it long enough, in a sense of... and I don't mean like a momentary hypnotic concentration or absorption or something.

143
00:15:15,103 --> 00:15:25,393
[SPEAKER_00]: It means if you cultivate that context whereby, instead of sensual desires, what you're bothered is thinking and pondering of the perceptions of sensuality, while you're fully withdrawn from it.

144
00:15:25,853 --> 00:15:32,160
[SPEAKER_00]: You stay long enough in that setup, in that context, you will be free from sensuality eventually.

145
00:15:32,520 --> 00:15:41,409
[SPEAKER_00]: But obviously, the Buddha, you know, trying to find the ultimate way out, the complete way out, leaving nothing unturned, he realized, okay, so is there higher than this?

146
00:15:43,952 --> 00:15:46,555
[SPEAKER_00]: So, is there higher than the second jhāna now?

147
00:15:49,772 --> 00:16:03,444
[SPEAKER_00]: And realize that the way to get higher was also to recognize now that the absence of thinking, the absence of sensual perceptions and thoughts that were beside him, well, that that too, actually, could be even more pleasant.

148
00:16:03,584 --> 00:16:06,686
[SPEAKER_00]: And from that point of view, as in more pleasant, more agreeable, more beneficial.

149
00:16:07,407 --> 00:16:10,710
[SPEAKER_00]: And from that point of view, you refer to it as an affliction.

150
00:16:13,113 --> 00:16:26,980
[SPEAKER_00]: So then he thought, why don't I, with the fading away of joy, abiding equanimous, recollected, comprehending, experiencing ease with the body, abide, having entered upon the third jhāna, with regard to which the noble ones say, one abides equanimous, recollected, and at ease.

151
00:16:27,480 --> 00:16:35,944
[SPEAKER_00]: But again, the mind didn't see the absence of that joy of second jhāna, when the thinking and pondering has been stilled, hasn't seen the joy of that as peaceful.

152
00:16:37,024 --> 00:16:40,026
[SPEAKER_00]: So, after some time, having seen the peril of that joy,

153
00:16:41,042 --> 00:16:42,803
[SPEAKER_00]: The implicit risk, he cultivated it.

154
00:16:42,903 --> 00:16:45,624
[SPEAKER_00]: Having realized the value of the absence of the joy, he fostered it.

155
00:16:45,744 --> 00:16:48,525
[SPEAKER_00]: Seeing it as peaceful, the mind started leaping towards that.

156
00:16:50,026 --> 00:16:52,627
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, he was abiding, having entered upon the third jhāna.

157
00:16:53,988 --> 00:16:59,590
[SPEAKER_00]: Having abided in that way, perceptions and attentions connected with the joy started to beset him.

158
00:16:59,650 --> 00:17:00,651
[SPEAKER_00]: He realized the affliction.

159
00:17:01,031 --> 00:17:01,831
[SPEAKER_00]: That's an affliction.

160
00:17:02,371 --> 00:17:03,832
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, repeats the same process.

161
00:17:04,813 --> 00:17:10,175
[SPEAKER_00]: Why don't I, with a giving up of ease and unease, end with the ending of those joys and upsets,

162
00:17:11,632 --> 00:17:19,421
[SPEAKER_00]: Surmount that domain and with the purity of equanimity and recollection, without ease or unease, abide having entered upon the fourth jhāna.

163
00:17:20,722 --> 00:17:24,006
[SPEAKER_00]: And it repeats the same after the fourth jhāna.

164
00:17:24,566 --> 00:17:30,533
[SPEAKER_00]: How about I surmount all the perceptions of resistance, but the mind doesn't see the extent of infinite space as peaceful.

165
00:17:32,642 --> 00:17:50,198
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he first comprehended the extent of infinite space, cultivated it, abandoned any residual views that they would have been preventing, basically, from him to... for himself, on the level of his mano, to realize that is peaceful.

166
00:17:50,939 --> 00:17:52,120
[SPEAKER_00]: And once that was all clear,

167
00:17:53,642 --> 00:18:03,245
[SPEAKER_00]: Doubt-free, protected that context until it sank in, down to that animal, which then also agreed and realized, oh, no, this is indeed peaceful, and leapt at it.

168
00:18:03,905 --> 00:18:08,527
[SPEAKER_00]: That's when he entered jhāna, or entered second jhāna, or third, or fourth, or infinite space.

169
00:18:11,067 --> 00:18:16,389
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then he went further, because he could see that, well, he could still continue surmounting whatever's in front of him.

170
00:18:16,509 --> 00:18:23,091
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why don't I, having fully surmounted the extent of infinite space, aware that consciousness is infinite, abide having entered that?

171
00:18:24,928 --> 00:18:26,689
[SPEAKER_00]: My mind didn't see the extent of it initially.

172
00:18:26,970 --> 00:18:27,690
[SPEAKER_00]: Later on, he did.

173
00:18:27,790 --> 00:18:28,531
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he entered it.

174
00:18:29,411 --> 00:18:34,135
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, he thought, why don't I, having fully surmounted the extent of infinite consciousness, aware that there is nothing?

175
00:18:35,476 --> 00:18:46,744
[SPEAKER_00]: So, pretty much, the domain, the extent of nothingness, taught his mind how to see it as peaceful, leapt at it, entered it.

176
00:18:47,945 --> 00:18:54,049
[SPEAKER_00]: Why don't I, having fully surmounted the extent of nothingness, abide, having entered upon the extent of neither perception or perception?

177
00:18:54,955 --> 00:18:55,195
[SPEAKER_00]: Same.

178
00:18:56,075 --> 00:18:57,436
[SPEAKER_00]: Until it sank in, he couldn't.

179
00:18:58,496 --> 00:19:06,079
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, when the mind fully understood the citta, fully understood that it is actually beneficial, then he leapt towards it.

180
00:19:06,480 --> 00:19:17,344
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, after a while, he realized that perceptions and attentions connected with that extent of nothingness are basically bothersome.

181
00:19:19,117 --> 00:19:20,558
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he thought, is there higher than this?

182
00:19:21,418 --> 00:19:24,800
[SPEAKER_00]: And he realized, yes, there is higher, which is cessation of perception and feeling.

183
00:19:25,981 --> 00:19:33,665
[SPEAKER_00]: After some time, surmounted the same, the mind leapt towards the cessation of perception and feeling, and he, he basically was abiding in it.

184
00:19:34,626 --> 00:19:37,147
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's when he realized that that's it.

185
00:19:37,307 --> 00:19:43,070
[SPEAKER_00]: There is absolutely nothing that can be underlied by influxes from this point onwards.

186
00:19:43,590 --> 00:19:47,853
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if somebody were to progressively develop all the way to the cessation of perception and feeling,

187
00:19:48,881 --> 00:19:57,164
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no, like, only now you have to spend time in it, so to speak, because if you come to that point, there will be no more influxes left underneath you, so to speak.

188
00:19:57,304 --> 00:19:59,284
[SPEAKER_00]: In the first jhāna, there still could be.

189
00:19:59,364 --> 00:20:00,705
[SPEAKER_00]: Second jhāna, there still could be.

190
00:20:00,725 --> 00:20:03,966
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why he said he did not claim his full enlightenment.

191
00:20:05,030 --> 00:20:06,151
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah, it's the next paragraph.

192
00:20:06,571 --> 00:20:19,658
[SPEAKER_00]: As long as I hadn't entered upon and withdrawn from these nine progressive attainments of abiding in both forward and reverse order, I did not announce my supreme perfect awakening in this world with its god Maras and Brahmas.

193
00:20:21,479 --> 00:20:24,020
[SPEAKER_00]: This population with its ascetics and Brahmins, its god and humans.

194
00:20:24,501 --> 00:20:28,063
[SPEAKER_00]: But when he has entered upon and withdrawn, fully understood,

195
00:20:29,322 --> 00:20:50,348
[SPEAKER_00]: This withdrawal principle, on account of seeing the danger, mind also accepting it, leaping towards the higher domain, then he did announce that his liberation of the mind is unshakable, last birth, there is absolutely nothing that can underlie it, nor āsavas and anusayas, like how they're known in other suttas.

196
00:20:57,079 --> 00:21:03,321
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, you know, a few obvious questions come up on the basis of this sutta.

197
00:21:04,301 --> 00:21:08,643
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, putting aside the subtle attainments, they just follow the same principle.

198
00:21:09,623 --> 00:21:26,249
[SPEAKER_00]: Also, you know, you can wrap your head around, say, the base of infinite space and consciousness is infinite and so on, but if you haven't actually at least withdrawn yourself from sensuality into a first jhāna,

199
00:21:29,256 --> 00:21:36,243
[SPEAKER_00]: It's pointless to even try to figure out those higher states, because it won't be those states you're figuring out.

200
00:21:36,924 --> 00:21:42,310
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if we go back to the very first one, which is sensualities and abyss,

201
00:21:45,443 --> 00:21:56,826
[SPEAKER_00]: How do you basically overcome that abyss and find the safety that's completely not dependent on the body or the flesh, how the suttas would say, i.e.

202
00:21:56,906 --> 00:21:57,907
[SPEAKER_00]: the five senses.

203
00:21:58,287 --> 00:21:59,887
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's completely withdrawn from it.

204
00:21:59,907 --> 00:22:11,250
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no point of contact with it, no dependence on any other, you know, slightly better sense object or similar, but it's truly withdrawn, truly safe,

205
00:22:12,133 --> 00:22:15,796
[SPEAKER_00]: Truly unshakable, from that point of view, which is what the first jhāna is.

206
00:22:16,997 --> 00:22:27,604
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the question is, like, then, can even a householder... is there a way for him to, eh... not experience renunciation as an abyss?

207
00:22:30,947 --> 00:22:32,568
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eh... of course, yes.

208
00:22:32,688 --> 00:22:38,873
[SPEAKER_00]: Initially, when you think about... if a householder would think about it, it's gonna be, eh... well, scary.

209
00:22:39,653 --> 00:22:40,714
[SPEAKER_00]: That's kind of the, eh...

210
00:22:42,626 --> 00:22:44,447
[SPEAKER_00]: The inevitable, say, outcome.

211
00:22:44,487 --> 00:22:55,935
[SPEAKER_00]: Somebody who hasn't practiced any restraint, hasn't heard about the necessity of the virtue and restraint, or heard it, like, on the level of, oh, this is something optional or conditional.

212
00:22:56,015 --> 00:22:56,315
[SPEAKER_00]: I could...

213
00:22:57,015 --> 00:23:00,898
[SPEAKER_00]: I could do it, you know, sometimes, but it's not... it's not the core of the practice.

214
00:23:00,998 --> 00:23:02,979
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not, like, the direction of the jhāna.

215
00:23:03,680 --> 00:23:06,662
[SPEAKER_00]: When they hear that they have to do it, yeah, they're gonna be upset.

216
00:23:07,917 --> 00:23:11,159
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they're going to blame everybody else except their own sensual desires.

217
00:23:11,199 --> 00:23:14,180
[SPEAKER_00]: They do define that abyss, because that's another thing, by the way.

218
00:23:15,001 --> 00:23:20,103
[SPEAKER_00]: The abyss of sensuality is not defined because it's actually or factually there an abyss.

219
00:23:20,243 --> 00:23:23,265
[SPEAKER_00]: It's defined by the strength of your desires towards those sense objects.

220
00:23:24,745 --> 00:23:30,028
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the stronger your desires, the bigger the abyss, if you were to even consider saying no to them.

221
00:23:31,590 --> 00:23:32,632
[SPEAKER_00]: But there's no other way around it.

222
00:23:33,012 --> 00:23:35,176
[SPEAKER_00]: As I said, there is no magical formula or a potion.

223
00:23:35,296 --> 00:23:36,418
[SPEAKER_00]: They will just do it for you.

224
00:23:37,280 --> 00:23:41,748
[SPEAKER_00]: So, is there a way for householder to eventually not experience the renunciation as abyss?

225
00:23:41,808 --> 00:23:42,329
[SPEAKER_00]: Absolutely.

226
00:23:43,427 --> 00:24:01,847
[SPEAKER_00]: gradual training in virtue, restraining, recognizing that level, as we speak about, with your intentions that are rooted in greed, aversion, distraction, taming that, not acting out of that, all of that is already shrinking the abyss.

227
00:24:05,784 --> 00:24:21,075
[SPEAKER_00]: But, see, in order to do that, you do need, like, on the level of your mano, on the level of your intellect, you need to start, actually, ponder and think about it, because it's like going against your entire existence, the grain of your existence.

228
00:24:21,215 --> 00:24:23,856
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're gonna need to have a pretty good reason to do it.

229
00:24:24,557 --> 00:24:33,023
[SPEAKER_00]: And, unless you would have really, like, sat down with it, repeatedly contemplated it, and so on, no amount of reasons will sink in.

230
00:24:35,395 --> 00:24:55,704
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for that, you do need to basically even tailor your environment, stop seeking mindless distraction and safety in the distraction through the environment.

231
00:24:56,004 --> 00:25:01,887
[SPEAKER_00]: So, all that needs to be reined in, honed in, and then protected, maintained.

232
00:25:02,810 --> 00:25:04,070
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the citta gets used to it.

233
00:25:04,150 --> 00:25:06,331
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you bring it closer, and then citta gets used to it.

234
00:25:06,351 --> 00:25:11,232
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't do it overnight, for sure, but the worst danger is to have the view that you don't have to do it.

235
00:25:11,533 --> 00:25:13,113
[SPEAKER_00]: And, you know, many people do have that view.

236
00:25:13,233 --> 00:25:17,354
[SPEAKER_00]: Even people that keep the virtue, they keep it in that, as I said, conditional sense.

237
00:25:17,414 --> 00:25:24,656
[SPEAKER_00]: I keep it because somebody said it supports my meditation, concentration, self-hypnotic practice.

238
00:25:25,216 --> 00:25:26,197
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's why I'm keeping it.

239
00:25:26,297 --> 00:25:30,138
[SPEAKER_00]: But I don't keep it as a form of I'm actually training my citta in this.

240
00:25:30,658 --> 00:25:32,920
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm recognizing my intentions, taking responsibility for it.

241
00:25:33,400 --> 00:25:36,362
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're not really keeping it.

242
00:25:37,162 --> 00:25:45,267
[SPEAKER_00]: So, initially, when somebody says no to their desires and they haven't done it before or haven't done it seriously, well, that abyss will probably even become bigger.

243
00:25:46,688 --> 00:25:54,073
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, again, the mind, the citta, will panic and it will try to to pressure you through fear and doubt to actually don't do it and go back to it.

244
00:25:55,237 --> 00:25:58,839
[SPEAKER_00]: But, as I said, like, there is absolutely no other way.

245
00:25:58,959 --> 00:26:01,600
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's even what this householder realized, Tapusa.

246
00:26:01,660 --> 00:26:09,504
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, oh, this is the very thing that differentiates everybody else from this teaching and discipline.

247
00:26:10,464 --> 00:26:13,806
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the sooner you start, the better, really.

248
00:26:14,486 --> 00:26:22,850
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, so, kind of, the next natural question would be, what would be the more specific practical things that a person who now

249
00:26:24,457 --> 00:26:25,438
[SPEAKER_00]: wants to train in this.

250
00:26:25,858 --> 00:26:34,745
[SPEAKER_00]: What can they do so that that citta will eventually... that wild animal will eventually leap towards renunciation itself?

251
00:26:34,825 --> 00:26:43,171
[SPEAKER_00]: And, as we said, virtue and restraint not negotiable, but it's important to to start emphasizing the right things, not all at once.

252
00:26:43,552 --> 00:26:47,415
[SPEAKER_00]: Just say no to everything and just lock yourself in the room and no distractions.

253
00:26:47,455 --> 00:26:51,178
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, that's... you could say

254
00:26:52,491 --> 00:27:01,188
[SPEAKER_00]: By some stretch of theory, if somebody really knows what they're doing, that could possibly work, but practically speaking, you're gonna overwhelm that citta.

255
00:27:02,210 --> 00:27:04,214
[SPEAKER_00]: So, take on the precepts,

256
00:27:05,295 --> 00:27:07,097
[SPEAKER_00]: Five precepts with celibacy.

257
00:27:07,338 --> 00:27:15,807
[SPEAKER_00]: Work towards it, but at the same time, yeah, don't neglect the sense of urgency in regard to it, because you might not have your whole life ahead of you.

258
00:27:15,827 --> 00:27:18,110
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, actually, you always have your whole life ahead of you.

259
00:27:18,130 --> 00:27:21,834
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just that that whole life might not last as long as you hope it will.

260
00:27:23,316 --> 00:27:36,263
[SPEAKER_00]: So, emphasizing the important, you know, the core convergence point of sensuality, the big things, and training in regard to that, and then refining it as the time passes, as the citta becomes more comfortable, and so on.

261
00:27:36,303 --> 00:27:44,387
[SPEAKER_00]: Not all at once thinking, if I just adhere to this blindly, as a form of ascetic practice, pretty much, the citta will magically become enlightened.

262
00:27:44,507 --> 00:27:44,648
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

263
00:27:45,428 --> 00:27:45,568
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

264
00:27:45,628 --> 00:27:48,470
[SPEAKER_00]: Understanding needs to be born out of your virtue and restraint.

265
00:27:49,530 --> 00:27:52,972
[SPEAKER_00]: And no amount of willpower will just magically create understanding.

266
00:27:54,688 --> 00:27:58,389
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in order to do that, you need to start prioritizing the right thing.

267
00:27:58,429 --> 00:28:16,175
[SPEAKER_00]: As I said already, you gotta pay attention to your environment and tailor it to the extent that you can, so that, you know, your citta and your senses are not simply overwhelmed... overwhelmed and bombarded with too many things that you just can't cope with.

268
00:28:17,676 --> 00:28:21,257
[SPEAKER_00]: So, eh... sorting out your priorities,

269
00:28:22,998 --> 00:28:30,061
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah, so one of you sent this in an email as a reply and I thought I'll just read it out, because it was a good list, a good checklist.

270
00:28:31,261 --> 00:28:46,187
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what a lay person should aim at doing is sorting out the priorities, which will certainly gonna cause some hassle in the beginning, due to doubt, uncertainty, fear, attachments, obligations and so on.

271
00:28:46,367 --> 00:28:48,768
[SPEAKER_00]: But you have to recognize, okay, my work begins with this.

272
00:28:51,298 --> 00:28:55,280
[SPEAKER_00]: Pondering around the question what comes first in your priorities.

273
00:28:55,360 --> 00:29:03,065
[SPEAKER_00]: So, is it more important to be liberated from sensuality and free from saṃsara once and for all, or your lay life satisfactions and joys?

274
00:29:03,845 --> 00:29:06,527
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a kind of simple choice, really.

275
00:29:06,587 --> 00:29:13,151
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not complex, but it's not going to be an easy choice to make, because although, even as the Buddha said, on the mental level,

276
00:29:14,531 --> 00:29:17,292
[SPEAKER_00]: He understood that, no, actually, renunciation is good.

277
00:29:17,852 --> 00:29:19,372
[SPEAKER_00]: Seclusion is excellent.

278
00:29:20,012 --> 00:29:22,373
[SPEAKER_00]: But the citta was like, no, no, I disagree with that.

279
00:29:22,433 --> 00:29:25,334
[SPEAKER_00]: I want this.

280
00:29:25,454 --> 00:29:31,395
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in order to then train it, as the next checklist says, you got to establish the faith, basically.

281
00:29:31,435 --> 00:29:35,676
[SPEAKER_00]: Establish the faith in these teachings, in a sense of to start applying it.

282
00:29:35,756 --> 00:29:39,637
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not going to have an immediate verification that sense restraint is good and seclusion is good.

283
00:29:40,037 --> 00:29:42,538
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you reason clearly enough,

284
00:29:43,553 --> 00:29:46,395
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you don't need some high intellect to understand these principles.

285
00:29:46,435 --> 00:29:49,878
[SPEAKER_00]: You just need some honesty, basically.

286
00:29:50,199 --> 00:29:52,621
[SPEAKER_00]: You would see that, well, it's certainly worth a try.

287
00:29:53,521 --> 00:30:00,747
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you decide to try, then you have to have faith that, like, okay, I gotta try this really and properly, not half-heartedly.

288
00:30:00,768 --> 00:30:09,155
[SPEAKER_00]: So, faith in the application of quite a straightforward non-intellectual instruction of training in virtue and precepts and sense restraint

289
00:30:10,687 --> 00:30:14,868
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's that's like a pretty much the necessary condition for somebody to start doing it.

290
00:30:16,528 --> 00:30:26,170
[SPEAKER_00]: So, gotta read the suttas, remind yourself, learn about different aspects of renunciation, clarify it as possible, so that you can actually be practicing.

291
00:30:28,390 --> 00:30:34,411
[SPEAKER_00]: The next checklist is, well, accordingly, you have to be then associating with the people of the same values.

292
00:30:35,331 --> 00:30:38,112
[SPEAKER_00]: Seek their advice, if you're in a position to do so, and follow it.

293
00:30:40,050 --> 00:30:41,471
[SPEAKER_00]: Practice dhāna and generosity.

294
00:30:42,472 --> 00:30:47,236
[SPEAKER_00]: And, of course, aim towards celibacy, if you're not already celibate.

295
00:30:48,137 --> 00:30:54,003
[SPEAKER_00]: And include that, because that's like one of the main exemplifications of the entire sensual domain.

296
00:30:57,241 --> 00:31:08,944
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, later on, say, you know, you're celibate, you're restrained, you could then start discerning the subtler layers of the five hindrances and how they manifest in different ways.

297
00:31:09,244 --> 00:31:15,326
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, you could start thinking about it even now, but first things first.

298
00:31:16,086 --> 00:31:21,107
[SPEAKER_00]: So, all of that, obviously, is predicated on watching your actions by body, speech and mind.

299
00:31:22,882 --> 00:31:46,992
[SPEAKER_00]: Behavior, as we spoke before, not allowing the center stage and so on, on the basis of withdrawal and from the unwholesome states and further, that's how the mind will eventually also dry up from those desires and that's pretty much the time when you would start seeing your own citta actually preferring it, actually leaping towards it.

300
00:31:48,693 --> 00:31:51,434
[SPEAKER_00]: So, as it was said already,

301
00:31:53,067 --> 00:32:07,080
[SPEAKER_00]: The only way out of this, it's not through, as I said, physical sensations, meditation techniques, astral projections, visualizations, or whatever your practice of meditation might be.

302
00:32:07,840 --> 00:32:13,786
[SPEAKER_00]: It's through one and only path of fully seeing the danger of the central domain that you're living in.

303
00:32:14,386 --> 00:32:14,627
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

304
00:32:15,848 --> 00:32:38,442
[SPEAKER_00]: If the Buddha himself, with the greatest paramis and everything, could not do it any other way, I would be, probably, as I said, you don't even need a great intellect and reasoning to see that, okay, so then, if he couldn't do it, who am I to talk about doing some other way, quicker way, or a different type of jhāna way.

305
00:32:38,882 --> 00:32:44,266
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, that's what I mean, like, if you're honest, you realize, okay, so, if he laid it out, and again, it's not

306
00:32:45,320 --> 00:32:46,561
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not hard to understand.

307
00:32:47,682 --> 00:32:51,525
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just hard to stick to it, because people would like faith in application.

308
00:32:52,426 --> 00:33:07,038
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you know, choose pretty much every other excuse to dismiss the application, or the the speaker who gave them that application, or the suttas, or the buddha, and do what they want to do, which is basically stay in the central domain, because they don't want to develop and face the abyss.

309
00:33:08,843 --> 00:33:14,027
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's, again, I mean, I've been already mentioning it, but it's good to say it clearly.

310
00:33:14,067 --> 00:33:26,637
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, when, you know, questions of could somebody still hypothetically practice concentration, you know, the way it's being taught today?

311
00:33:27,738 --> 00:33:35,444
[SPEAKER_00]: Can they practice concentration to encourage, you know, to speed up, to speed up the leaping of the citta towards renunciation faster?

312
00:33:35,484 --> 00:33:37,846
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's an obvious categorical absolutely no.

313
00:33:38,976 --> 00:33:45,699
[SPEAKER_00]: Because whatever you are concentrating will be the object of your own mano and it won't be the citta behind it.

314
00:33:45,799 --> 00:33:59,084
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if there would be a magical object that can take the, you know, the citta in the background, the animal that you're not even seeing, towards renunciation, well, the Buddha would have outlined it.

315
00:34:00,544 --> 00:34:07,647
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the closer to the object of your concentration you can get is the object of your thoughts, of thinking and pondering about the peril

316
00:34:08,810 --> 00:34:09,530
[SPEAKER_00]: of sensuality.

317
00:34:12,832 --> 00:34:20,936
[SPEAKER_00]: If people try and argue and say, well, you know, the the physical sensations are not sensual, how can they not be sensual if they come from your body?

318
00:34:22,237 --> 00:34:24,398
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is one giant field of touch.

319
00:34:27,320 --> 00:34:28,760
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter how you try to spin it.

320
00:34:29,261 --> 00:34:30,521
[SPEAKER_00]: Or breath is not sensual.

321
00:34:30,741 --> 00:34:32,142
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it felt in the body?

322
00:34:32,222 --> 00:34:33,003
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

323
00:34:33,043 --> 00:34:35,484
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, even the suttas say breath is inseparable from the body.

324
00:34:35,564 --> 00:34:37,345
[SPEAKER_00]: It's bodily, that's why it's kāyā-saṅkhāra.

325
00:34:38,594 --> 00:34:41,456
[SPEAKER_00]: If it's connected with your body, it's connected with the flesh.

326
00:34:42,517 --> 00:34:43,838
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no other way around it.

327
00:34:45,220 --> 00:34:47,321
[SPEAKER_00]: If people say, well, it doesn't harm anybody.

328
00:34:47,802 --> 00:34:49,703
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, lots of things don't harm anybody.

329
00:34:50,124 --> 00:34:53,546
[SPEAKER_00]: Self-pleasuring doesn't harm anybody, technically, yet.

330
00:34:54,267 --> 00:34:58,471
[SPEAKER_00]: We all know how unwholesome that would be from somebody who wants to be celibate and keep the precepts.

331
00:34:59,428 --> 00:35:10,275
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's just, as I said, if you are honest, you will see that the reasoning in defending sensations or breath meditations of pleasure, they will take your mind away from... it doesn't hold up.

332
00:35:12,697 --> 00:35:23,864
[SPEAKER_00]: And when the Buddha did teach ānāpānasati, it was on the level of understanding and using the breath to outline your own body, from which you are pretty much already thoroughly or sufficiently withdrawn.

333
00:35:24,947 --> 00:35:44,162
[SPEAKER_00]: Instead of concentrating, instead of anesthetizing yourself basically through the common practices of meditation, invest the effort in calming down your environment, calming down your engagements with it, calming down the pulls and pressures that you experience on account of it,

334
00:35:45,072 --> 00:35:53,715
[SPEAKER_00]: Go and sit down quietly, but not out of that duty that as a Buddhist I must do this concentration because otherwise I feel like I don't know what I'm doing.

335
00:35:54,696 --> 00:36:04,439
[SPEAKER_00]: And sit down quietly and then think and ponder to the full extent of why sensuality is dangerous and why your own mind doesn't want to turn away from it.

336
00:36:06,040 --> 00:36:08,361
[SPEAKER_00]: And do it pretty much until the mind turns away from it.

337
00:36:08,721 --> 00:36:09,821
[SPEAKER_00]: And that would be your jhāna.

338
00:36:12,022 --> 00:36:13,683
[SPEAKER_00]: So the only way to encourage your citta

339
00:36:16,884 --> 00:36:34,137
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's why I said the first reason the citta is not turning towards it is because you yourself have not clarified and understood the benefits for yourself on the level of your own mind, on the level of your own intellect.

340
00:36:39,288 --> 00:36:41,830
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, so let's say somebody does develop that householder.

341
00:36:42,170 --> 00:36:43,591
[SPEAKER_00]: Perfectly, perfectly possible.

342
00:36:44,271 --> 00:36:54,799
[SPEAKER_00]: If he's celibate and withdrawn, and he enters the first jhāna, then as other householders in the suttas existed, became skilled in all four jhānas, because following the same progression.

343
00:36:55,619 --> 00:37:07,708
[SPEAKER_00]: And also, you know, once, once say, like, you abandon, you surmount the sensual domain, yes, it will require that diligence of bringing up the context of the peril of the thinking and pondering of the first jhāna, that's being besetted by the perceptions of sensuality and so on.

344
00:37:08,968 --> 00:37:12,849
[SPEAKER_00]: But, it won't be as much of a chore as it was overcoming the abyss.

345
00:37:14,530 --> 00:37:19,331
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why, like, you know, the faith would have already been thoroughly confirmed by then.

346
00:37:20,011 --> 00:37:31,094
[SPEAKER_00]: So, once somebody's in the first jhāna, they can easily continue practicing the same thing and get even closer to that full enlightenment and removal of all the underlying tendencies and so on.

347
00:37:32,580 --> 00:37:42,490
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the principle between that progression, I already described it when we read the sutta, and it's the one... you can say you progress upwards only through surmounting what's in front.

348
00:37:44,051 --> 00:37:51,678
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is absolutely no other way to it, or other shortcut, or similar.

349
00:37:51,799 --> 00:37:57,304
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this might bring up an idea of, like, you know, the foreground and the peripheral in your mind.

350
00:37:59,882 --> 00:38:01,903
[SPEAKER_00]: And, theoretically, that is true.

351
00:38:02,423 --> 00:38:21,310
[SPEAKER_00]: But, practically, foreground and the peripheral, say, when you think about it, will always carry an implicit intention, there and then, for you, that would be implying that I must surmount the foreground by including all the peripheral.

352
00:38:22,250 --> 00:38:24,151
[SPEAKER_00]: And that intention needs to be included.

353
00:38:25,351 --> 00:38:28,052
[SPEAKER_00]: But, see, that's not something you can just attend as a foreground.

354
00:38:28,993 --> 00:38:37,924
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yes, foreground and the peripheral, whatever that is, at whatever stage you might be, and then recognize your... the implicit imperative in it.

355
00:38:38,584 --> 00:38:46,714
[SPEAKER_00]: I must surmount this and include it, not follow it to surmount it, to surmount foreground and peripheral, because then you're still acting out of hindrance.

356
00:38:49,562 --> 00:38:51,503
[SPEAKER_00]: But, obviously, a lot more can be said about that.

357
00:38:51,623 --> 00:38:53,684
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe we can do another another sutta reading.

358
00:38:56,004 --> 00:39:12,050
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, somebody asked, would it be correct to say that the mind cannot understand, the citta cannot understand the peril of a less wholesome state and the value in a more wholesome state without being actually fully, thoroughly, first withdrawn from the less unwholesome state?

359
00:39:12,210 --> 00:39:12,470
[SPEAKER_00]: And yes.

360
00:39:13,130 --> 00:39:14,250
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, that's absolutely correct.

361
00:39:15,291 --> 00:39:18,552
[SPEAKER_00]: And that withdrawal is the necessary basis

362
00:39:20,175 --> 00:39:27,540
[SPEAKER_00]: For actual successful and productive understanding and contemplation of... Oh, sensuality is bad.

363
00:39:27,720 --> 00:39:32,043
[SPEAKER_00]: Or like, oh, yeah, thinking and pondering is a nuisance, from the point of view of the second jhāna.

364
00:39:32,503 --> 00:39:35,085
[SPEAKER_00]: So, first, you need to withdraw yourself from sensuality.

365
00:39:36,265 --> 00:39:36,746
[SPEAKER_00]: Physically.

366
00:39:37,166 --> 00:39:38,267
[SPEAKER_00]: Stop giving into it.

367
00:39:39,227 --> 00:39:43,790
[SPEAKER_00]: In order that you first be able to understand how it's not beneficial and how it's perilous.

368
00:39:44,471 --> 00:39:48,153
[SPEAKER_00]: In order for, then, that knowledge to sink down to the citta.

369
00:39:50,246 --> 00:39:56,750
[SPEAKER_00]: As we said before, you can't contemplate the peril of sensuality unless you're first withdrawn from sensuality.

370
00:39:57,190 --> 00:40:17,222
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just going to stay on a completely abstract level, and it can actually lead you astray even more, because while engaging with sensuality, you think you're contemplating the peril, and in other words, that which could have actually served as a peril, becomes something you're attracted to now, because you've been acting towards it while you were thinking, thinking the asubha thoughts and so on.

371
00:40:18,668 --> 00:40:28,715
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the peril is on the level of the core of sensuality, the core of giving in to those desires of that wild animal, is what keeps the wild animal wild.

372
00:40:29,756 --> 00:40:42,945
[SPEAKER_00]: And any pain you would have experienced, any pain you will experience, any pain that you could imagine you could experience, any suffering, basically, any pain, I mean any dukkha, that is the sole reason for it.

373
00:40:44,326 --> 00:40:46,567
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the wilderness of that animal, i.e.

374
00:40:46,708 --> 00:40:47,228
[SPEAKER_00]: craving.

375
00:40:48,943 --> 00:40:58,026
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not the circumstances, it's not the highway robbers chopping your limbs, it's not losing the loved ones, it's...

376
00:40:58,086 --> 00:41:00,687
[SPEAKER_00]: The sole thing is the wilderness of that animal.

377
00:41:02,287 --> 00:41:08,149
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eh... once you are very clear about that, the abyss will disappear.

378
00:41:09,204 --> 00:41:14,886
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, you might still get careless, as some of the noble disciples did in the suttas, because the abyss has disappeared.

379
00:41:15,526 --> 00:41:22,208
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, to that extent, the animal has seen, oh, that's where all the problems come from.

380
00:41:22,768 --> 00:41:34,512
[SPEAKER_00]: You chase after sensuality, you protect what's yours, so to experience that peace, that peace which always is undermined by your very chase, by the very pressure towards the chase, by the craving behind it.

381
00:41:36,140 --> 00:41:42,424
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's like the most direct thing, eh... direct principle behind the peril of sensuality that you can see.

382
00:41:42,504 --> 00:41:48,768
[SPEAKER_00]: But, as I said, one thing is you... you hear this, you think about it, you make sense of it, and another thing is to actually start doing it.

383
00:41:50,329 --> 00:42:04,818
[SPEAKER_00]: So, eh... in order to... to... to discern the domain of sensuality, in order for mind to leap away from it, you need to not just

384
00:42:06,136 --> 00:42:12,419
[SPEAKER_00]: Stop giving in to the contradictory acts of following your desires or real will and so on.

385
00:42:12,859 --> 00:42:25,304
[SPEAKER_00]: You also have to stop resisting what comes up when you sort of restrain your mind, when you round up, when you say no to desires, the pressures, the abyss that come up.

386
00:42:25,444 --> 00:42:30,346
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to stop resisting it as well, in a sense of you have to stop being averse towards it.

387
00:42:31,067 --> 00:42:34,368
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, again, that keeps you connected to it.

388
00:42:36,363 --> 00:42:44,526
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why the whole non-resistance, towards foreground and peripheral, and the intention to act out of it, needs to be thoroughly developed.

389
00:42:45,847 --> 00:42:54,750
[SPEAKER_00]: But, see, that non-resistance that we talk about, enduring on the right level, we talk about, which fosters that resistance, that can only be done once you round up the mind.

390
00:42:55,873 --> 00:42:59,136
[SPEAKER_00]: Once you actually do have some aggregate of virtue and sense restraint.

391
00:42:59,877 --> 00:43:08,003
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, you know, if you don't, saying, oh, I won't resist anything, basically, almost automatically means, oh, you go back to sensuality.

392
00:43:08,844 --> 00:43:09,104
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no.

393
00:43:09,144 --> 00:43:17,531
[SPEAKER_00]: You gotta resist things because the initial starting point is too much of of basically flowing, going along with the flow.

394
00:43:17,852 --> 00:43:19,273
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you gotta resist the flow.

395
00:43:19,313 --> 00:43:21,655
[SPEAKER_00]: You gotta resist the cover, the current.

396
00:43:23,330 --> 00:43:27,592
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, the result of that, okay, now I should not resist that.

397
00:43:28,292 --> 00:43:33,314
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you find the middle way, the balance, so to speak.

398
00:43:33,514 --> 00:43:41,677
[SPEAKER_00]: So, and then, yes, somebody also asked, like, so, you know, technically speaking, one could leave the household life today,

399
00:43:42,902 --> 00:44:04,036
[SPEAKER_00]: Become a monk, give up, you know, the household life, belongings, family and so on, but not necessarily renounce sensuality on the level of that desires, exactly, or still resist it and think that just through doubling down in the resistance and ascetic practices and following the the sense of duty or whatever else, they will just power through it.

400
00:44:04,236 --> 00:44:05,136
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, no.

401
00:44:05,396 --> 00:44:05,557
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

402
00:44:06,674 --> 00:44:20,361
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if somebody, say, technically somebody could be a monk, would have been, or a nun, given up household life, given up family, and still very much remain connected to sensuality, which means still very much be exposed to that abyss.

403
00:44:23,382 --> 00:44:25,423
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it still hasn't been been addressed.

404
00:44:26,084 --> 00:44:32,267
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind still hasn't been trained to get used to it, stop fearing it, stop being anxious in the face of it,

405
00:44:34,461 --> 00:44:37,663
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, it's always going to be there as a potential.

406
00:44:37,703 --> 00:44:41,745
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even if you'd never experienced abyss, it's because you never said no to those desires on that level.

407
00:44:43,426 --> 00:44:44,427
[SPEAKER_00]: Or because you're an arahant.

408
00:44:44,467 --> 00:44:46,208
[SPEAKER_00]: So, which one of those two is?

409
00:44:46,368 --> 00:44:47,048
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you can guess.

410
00:44:48,509 --> 00:44:51,731
[SPEAKER_00]: Pretty much covers the basic principles of this sutta.

411
00:44:51,751 --> 00:44:52,932
[SPEAKER_00]: As I said, I didn't want to go into

412
00:44:54,522 --> 00:45:10,835
[SPEAKER_00]: Neither perceptions, no perceptions, and so on, and just inundate you with theoretical ideas and knowledges, while the most practical principle that sits right in front of your face could be developed with the actual fruits and the benefit of it.

413
00:45:10,855 --> 00:45:13,257
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why I choose to stick to that.

